# Dependency vet / advisory-scanning posture (docs/09 T12, control 3:
# "cargo-vet/advisory scanning").
#
# `tools/governance/check_dependency_audit.py` reads this file and fails closed
# unless every externally-sourced package `cargo` resolves in `Cargo.lock` has
# exactly one matching `[[audits.<name>]]` entry, for the *exact* version and
# the *exact* checksum Cargo.lock pins. A dependency that is new, or whose
# version or checksum changed since it was last vetted, has no matching entry
# and fails the check until a human adds one — the fail-closed property T12
# asks for. An entry for a version or checksum `Cargo.lock` no longer resolves
# is a stale entry and also fails, so a superseded audit cannot linger and be
# mistaken for coverage of what actually ships today.
#
# This is deliberately a smaller shape than upstream `cargo-vet`'s own
# `audits.toml`: no delta audits (every entry is a full audit of the exact
# version named, never a diff from a prior version — stricter than upstream,
# not weaker, and simpler to check mechanically), no imported third-party audit
# sets, and no `criteria.toml` — the two criteria this file uses are defined
# once, here:
#
#   safe-to-deploy
#       Reviewed against upstream cargo-vet's own "safe-to-deploy": no obvious
#       backdoor, no unreviewed unsafe that isn't inherent to the crate's
#       documented purpose, no ambient network or filesystem access outside
#       what the dependency-rationale entry describes. Required for anything
#       that reaches the shipped artifact (kind `normal`).
#   safe-to-run
#       The weaker bar: safe to *execute* during the build (a build script may
#       shell out or read the filesystem) but never linked into a shipped
#       artifact. Required for `build`/`dev`-only edges.
#
# Why a `checksum` field upstream `cargo-vet` does not have: upstream trusts
# its own imported/vendored source tree, hashed independently of `Cargo.lock`.
# This workspace vendors nothing and fetches nothing at check time (a gate
# that phones home is nondeterministic — see README-gov1.md and this file's
# own checker's module docstring), so the only artifact identity available to
# bind an audit to is the one `Cargo.lock` itself already pins: the package's
# registry checksum. Recording it here and checking it against `Cargo.lock`
# means a same-version, different-bytes substitution — the checksum changing
# under an unchanged version string — is caught exactly as loudly as a version
# bump is. It is *not* an independent re-hash of the crate; it certifies that
# whoever performed the audit saw the same bytes `cargo` will fetch, nothing
# more.
#
# Refreshing this file is a human action, never a gate action: bump a
# dependency, then before `Cargo.lock`'s new version/checksum can pass this
# check, someone has to read what changed and add a new full-audit entry
# recording who did that and what they found. That friction is the point.
#
# `who`: every entry so far was reviewed by `continuum-dev` from the published
# crate documentation and source characteristics (no live network fetch was
# used to write this file, consistent with the checker's own no-network rule);
# a future refresh is free to name a different reviewer.

[[audits.arrayref]]
version = "0.3.9"
checksum = "76a2e8124351fda1ef8aaaa3bbd7ebbcb486bbcd4225aca0aa0d84bb2db8fecb"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
Three-macro crate (`array_ref!`, `array_refs!`, `mut_array_refs!`) turning a
slice into a reference to a fixed-size array without copying. The unsafe it
contains is exactly its documented purpose — a bounds-checked-length pointer
cast to `&[T; N]` — with no I/O, no build script, and no platform-conditional
code path. blake3 uses it in its internal buffer plumbing; nothing here reads
the environment, the network, or the filesystem.
"""

[[audits.arrayvec]]
version = "0.7.8"
checksum = "d3fb67a6e08acf24fdeccbac2cb6ac4305825bd1f117462e0e6f2f193345ad56"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
Fixed-capacity, stack-allocated `Vec`/`String` substitutes. One of the more
heavily used and reviewed small crates in the ecosystem; its unsafe is
uninitialized-memory management inherent to a no-alloc growable buffer, scoped
to its own internal representation. No build script, no I/O, no ambient state.
Used here with default features off per the workspace's blake3 pin.
"""

[[audits.blake3]]
version = "1.8.5"
checksum = "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
The cryptographic hash behind continuum-value's ContentHasher seam
(dependency-rationale.toml has the full TCB rationale; this entry is the vet
half). Pinned with `default-features = false, features = ["pure"]`, which
drops `std` and keeps the vendored `cc` build-time edge dormant (blake3's own
build.rs never invokes a C compiler under `pure`). `blake3_matches_the_
published_test_vectors` in continuum-value pins the specification's own test
vectors as literals, so a future version that changed the function's output
would fail that test independently of this entry.
"""

[[audits.cc]]
version = "1.4.0"
checksum = "5add81bb678e6cb321aff7fa0dc7689ad82b112dbc032cea19f91d6b8e3582b9"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-run"]
notes = """
Build-only edge (blake3 declares it unconditionally; the workspace's `pure`
feature choice means its C-compiler-invoking path is never exercised — see the
`blake3` entry and dependency-rationale.toml). `cc` genuinely can shell out to
a C toolchain and is one of the largest crates in this closure, which is why
it gets `safe-to-run` rather than a blanket pass: it never links into a
shipped artifact of this workspace, so the bar is "safe to execute at build
time," not "safe to ship."
"""

[[audits.cfg-if]]
version = "1.0.4"
checksum = "9330f8b2ff13f34540b44e946ef35111825727b38d33286ef986142615121801"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
A single `cfg_if!` macro expanding to ordinary `cfg`-gated item selection, from
the rust-lang project itself. No unsafe, no build script, no runtime behavior
of its own — it exists at compile time and disappears.
"""

[[audits.constant_time_eq]]
version = "0.4.2"
checksum = "3d52eff69cd5e647efe296129160853a42795992097e8af39800e1060caeea9b"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
Constant-time byte-slice comparison, used by blake3 to avoid a timing side
channel in its own internal comparisons. Small, single-purpose, no build
script, no I/O; a defect here would weaken a timing property blake3 relies on
but cannot change what bytes continuum-value's hash seam produces.
"""

[[audits.cpufeatures]]
version = "0.3.0"
checksum = "8b2a41393f66f16b0823bb79094d54ac5fbd34ab292ddafb9a0456ac9f87d201"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
RustCrypto runtime CPU-feature detection, reached only on aarch64/loongarch
targets per dependency-rationale.toml's `cargo tree` note; on the x86-64
target this workspace is built and gated on, blake3 dispatches through
`std::arch` feature detection instead and this crate contributes no code to
the build. Reviewed for the platforms it does apply to: no build script, no
network, and its own `libc` edge is read-only `/proc`-style feature probing.
"""

[[audits.find-msvc-tools]]
version = "0.1.9"
checksum = "5baebc0774151f905a1a2cc41989300b1e6fbb29aff0ceffa1064fdd3088d582"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-run"]
notes = """
Windows-only MSVC toolchain path discovery, spun out of `cc`'s own internals
and reached only through `cc`'s build-time closure. `safe-to-run`, matching
`cc`: it never ships in an artifact of this workspace and this workspace does
not target MSVC today, so it contributes nothing to a non-Windows build.
"""

[[audits.libc]]
version = "0.2.189"
checksum = "3eaf3ede3fee6db1a4c2ee091bf8a8b4dccdc6d17f656fb07896ee72867612f2"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-deploy"]
notes = """
The canonical low-level FFI declaration crate maintained by the rust-lang
project, reached here only through `cpufeatures` on aarch64/loongarch. It is
almost entirely `extern "C"` type and constant declarations with no logic of
its own to review; the unsafety lives at each crate's call sites, not here.
"""

[[audits.shlex]]
version = "2.0.1"
checksum = "f8fadd59c855ef2080decdef8ff161eb6661b86933c9d82e5ba29dc602a55aba"
who = "continuum-dev"
criteria = ["safe-to-run"]
notes = """
Shell-token quoting/splitting used by `cc` to parse compiler flag environment
variables at build time. `safe-to-run`, matching `cc`: build-time only, no
network, no unsafe, never linked into a shipped artifact of this workspace.
"""
